WEBVTT

NOTE Skylab: Space Station I (1973) - Riah TV captions

00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:04.500
[music]

00:00:30.460 --> 00:00:33.980
I think it's a natural
question is, why explore space?

00:00:35.370 --> 00:00:36.750
And some people have,

00:00:38.020 --> 00:00:40.320
different people have
different views on this.

00:00:40.480 --> 00:00:43.750
Personally, to my way of
thinking, part of human nature

00:00:43.800 --> 00:00:45.620
is to reach out and explore.

00:00:47.980 --> 00:00:50.930
The fact of the matter is, if
man stops really stretching

00:00:50.980 --> 00:00:54.560
himself and extending himself
and looking, looking out, then I

00:00:54.610 --> 00:00:57.470
think that's when civilization
will begin to decline.

00:00:58.410 --> 00:01:01.120
Man just has that inquisitive
nature and it's got to be

00:01:01.170 --> 00:01:03.150
satisfied. Right

00:01:05.780 --> 00:01:11.770
on the threshold of really a
brand new opportunity to explore

00:01:11.820 --> 00:01:15.090
the solar system and the
universe and to increase the

00:01:15.140 --> 00:01:17.080
value of benefits
back here on home.

00:01:19.460 --> 00:01:23.610
Space lab itself is presently
conceived, will provide us with

00:01:23.660 --> 00:01:27.290
a capability which grows on
Skylab, expands on Skylab,

00:01:27.340 --> 00:01:29.810
provides us with an enormous
capability to conduct research

00:01:29.860 --> 00:01:31.440
in space. And,

00:01:33.320 --> 00:01:37.470
of course, space exploration
provides this spiritual quality.

00:01:37.520 --> 00:01:42.240
It allows you to go to the
unknown and find what's there,

00:01:42.290 --> 00:01:45.340
and hopefully by doing so,
you're going to improve your

00:01:45.390 --> 00:01:46.690
lot.

00:01:48.210 --> 00:01:52.280
They have a guy there to change
film, to change programs, to re

00:01:52.330 --> 00:01:55.560
-point the thing, to fix it when
it breaks, to take it out and

00:01:55.610 --> 00:01:56.930
put a new one in,

00:01:57.660 --> 00:02:03.470
is in many cases not only the
rewarding thing to do, but it's

00:02:03.520 --> 00:02:04.820
the cheap thing to do.

00:02:06.580 --> 00:02:10.330
First, we've explored the moon,
and we're now going to explore

00:02:10.380 --> 00:02:12.630
near space, and finally, we're
going to explore the solar

00:02:12.680 --> 00:02:16.160
system, all of this man
-placing integral part.

00:02:18.330 --> 00:02:21.180
If our children and our
children's children are going to

00:02:21.230 --> 00:02:23.860
enjoy the same quality of life
here on Earth as we have enjoyed

00:02:23.910 --> 00:02:26.800
in the past, we're going to
have to learn how to find new

00:02:26.850 --> 00:02:29.220
resources and how to manage
the ones that we have more

00:02:29.270 --> 00:02:30.830
efficiently and
more effectively.

00:02:33.210 --> 00:02:36.960
Of course, Apollo
went on to take

00:02:38.050 --> 00:02:41.600
us to the moon, but it also left
us with a tremendous technology

00:02:41.650 --> 00:02:43.350
to look towards the future.

00:02:43.480 --> 00:02:47.590
And as far as Skylab is
concerned, it represents a

00:02:47.640 --> 00:02:49.020
definite turning point.

00:03:12.300 --> 00:03:13.600
May

00:03:19.960 --> 00:03:22.320
14, 1973,

00:03:23.050 --> 00:03:27.460
a working laboratory. Man's
first home in space, on its way

00:03:27.510 --> 00:03:28.810
to Earth orbit.

00:03:28.870 --> 00:03:32.540
There to be occupied by three
teams of astronauts in three

00:03:32.590 --> 00:03:34.090
record -breaking missions.

00:03:42.120 --> 00:03:45.710
Over the next several months, a
lot of theory would become fact

00:03:45.760 --> 00:03:50.380
and give rise to new theory
about man, about Earth, about

00:03:50.430 --> 00:03:51.340
the physical universe.

00:03:51.390 --> 00:03:57.170
But on this day, a sequence of
events began to cast doubt on

00:03:57.220 --> 00:03:58.680
even a first mission.

00:03:59.740 --> 00:04:01.970
Of course, as soon as we could
listen in on the flight director

00:04:02.020 --> 00:04:05.350
net, we realized something had
happened to the lab, even though

00:04:05.400 --> 00:04:07.870
it looked like a perfect launch
from where we had been observing

00:04:07.920 --> 00:04:08.910
it.

00:04:08.960 --> 00:04:12.930
The meteoroid shield ripped
off about 60 seconds into the

00:04:12.980 --> 00:04:16.090
flight, and with the meteoroid
shield gone, this gave us the

00:04:16.140 --> 00:04:19.300
problem of overheating
in the workshop.

00:04:19.350 --> 00:04:25.680
so many different ideas in the
intervening 10 days were put

00:04:25.730 --> 00:04:29.820
out as different means of being
able to rig a temporary heat

00:04:29.870 --> 00:04:33.300
shield or a permanent heat
shield which would allow us to

00:04:33.350 --> 00:04:34.650
salvage the vehicle.

00:04:35.090 --> 00:04:37.720
Of course, all the NASA centers
were working on it, all our

00:04:37.770 --> 00:04:41.460
contractors were working on it,
and taking an active part in

00:04:41.510 --> 00:04:44.260
discussions and the decisions
that were made as to what's

00:04:44.310 --> 00:04:46.510
wrong with the workshop and
how we're going to fix it.

00:04:46.560 --> 00:04:49.610
We went to Marshall to work in
the water tank with the proposed

00:04:49.660 --> 00:04:52.590
fixes, talked to the engineers,
we're working on it, talked to

00:04:52.640 --> 00:04:54.990
the flight planners, and
discussed it, of course, with

00:04:55.040 --> 00:04:56.340
management.

00:04:56.700 --> 00:05:00.610
We actually launched on the 25th
of May with three potential ways

00:05:00.660 --> 00:05:03.920
of covering the workshop
to shade it from the sun.

00:05:09.820 --> 00:05:12.570
About seven and a half hours
after liftoff, as we were

00:05:12.620 --> 00:05:16.460
rendezvousing with the workshop,
the extent of the damage became

00:05:16.510 --> 00:05:17.960
apparent to us very rapidly.

00:05:18.010 --> 00:05:22.120
We first confirmed that the one
solar wing had completely left

00:05:22.170 --> 00:05:24.300
the vehicle and was
no longer around.

00:05:24.350 --> 00:05:27.420
We could see the damaged
wires at the hinge line.

00:05:27.470 --> 00:05:30.560
And, of course, all the gold
foil, which normally would have

00:05:30.610 --> 00:05:34.940
been underneath the heat shield
and meteoroid shield, was

00:05:34.990 --> 00:05:39.120
exposed to the sunlight, and
it had darkened on the sun side

00:05:39.170 --> 00:05:40.470
considerably.

00:05:46.450 --> 00:05:49.800
Then as we got around to the
side where the jammed solar wing

00:05:49.850 --> 00:05:53.840
was, it became immediately
obvious to us that this little

00:05:53.890 --> 00:05:57.380
tiny metal strap that had
torn off the heat shield and

00:05:57.430 --> 00:06:00.710
meteorite shield had
jammed the solar wing.

00:06:04.700 --> 00:06:07.740
Well, the advantages which
manned spaceflight offers are in

00:06:07.790 --> 00:06:09.980
the area of repair and
maintenance, which we've

00:06:10.030 --> 00:06:12.730
demonstrated time and
time again on Skylab.

00:06:13.260 --> 00:06:15.890
As a matter of fact, the whole
Skylab series of flights would

00:06:15.940 --> 00:06:18.230
have been a failure had it not
been for the ability of the

00:06:18.280 --> 00:06:20.380
first crew to repair
the space station.

00:06:23.270 --> 00:06:26.180
Pete and I went down into the
workshop, installed the canister

00:06:26.230 --> 00:06:29.530
that contained the parasol
in a scientific airlock.

00:06:30.520 --> 00:06:33.330
Joe was observing the deployment
of the parasol from the command

00:06:33.380 --> 00:06:34.680
module.

00:06:37.820 --> 00:06:42.290
I couldn't see the airlock
itself, but I could see this

00:06:42.340 --> 00:06:47.160
funny looking orange thing
all rolled up and skinny come

00:06:47.210 --> 00:06:49.050
poochin' out of there.

00:06:49.600 --> 00:06:51.510
And then Pete said, okay,
we're gonna let her go.

00:06:51.560 --> 00:06:55.510
And it just started to unfold
and did a lot of flopping and it

00:06:55.560 --> 00:06:58.370
kind of settled halfway
folded and halfway unfolded.

00:06:58.420 --> 00:07:01.670
We deployed it as best we could,
pulled it back down to us about

00:07:01.720 --> 00:07:04.890
a foot off and that was it.

00:07:04.940 --> 00:07:06.960
It worked quite well for
the rest of the mission.

00:07:07.390 --> 00:07:10.440
I think on about day four or
five we definitely knew that it

00:07:10.490 --> 00:07:12.300
was no longer an
uncomfortable environment.

00:07:12.350 --> 00:07:14.720
It was warm but certainly
nothing that you couldn't put up

00:07:14.770 --> 00:07:16.070
with.

00:07:27.200 --> 00:07:31.670
Of course not having gotten this
wing out on our first attempt

00:07:31.720 --> 00:07:36.300
when we came up there, we really
had about half of the electrical

00:07:36.350 --> 00:07:38.450
capability that we
should have had.

00:07:38.890 --> 00:07:42.500
So it was very very important
to attempt to get that GM solar

00:07:42.550 --> 00:07:43.850
panel out.

00:07:46.550 --> 00:07:48.740
I think we probably wouldn't
have had any Skylab missions had

00:07:48.790 --> 00:07:51.100
it not been for the fact that
man was able to get to the

00:07:51.150 --> 00:07:54.500
Skylab and to make it habitable
and to repair it in such a way

00:07:54.550 --> 00:07:56.850
that all of the missions
were completely successful.

00:07:58.080 --> 00:08:01.370
So Joe tried and then we decided
to tighten it up again and I'd

00:08:01.420 --> 00:08:04.490
try and I got under
it and boy, it broke.

00:08:04.540 --> 00:08:08.270
It let go in one big hurry and
there I was, launched into space

00:08:08.320 --> 00:08:11.530
again, free -floating, hanging
on the end of the string and

00:08:11.580 --> 00:08:13.930
well, by the time I got
straightened out again and

00:08:13.980 --> 00:08:16.720
looked around, why there
it was, out and deployed.

00:08:17.000 --> 00:08:20.460
Thus, very early, man's
presence proved to be vital.

00:08:20.800 --> 00:08:23.240
It would be so in the
missions that followed.

00:08:24.740 --> 00:08:28.210
But in areas where you need
man's flexibility and his

00:08:28.260 --> 00:08:32.850
initiative, for instance, in the
deployment of the solar wing on

00:08:32.900 --> 00:08:36.810
the first man mission, and on
the deployment of the sail that

00:08:36.860 --> 00:08:39.910
was used to shade, provide
thermal shading for the workshop

00:08:39.960 --> 00:08:43.180
on the second mission, And on
our mission where we had to

00:08:43.230 --> 00:08:48.040
resurface the coolant system or
where we had to actually get out

00:08:48.090 --> 00:08:51.260
and unjam or move the filter
wheel and the solar telescope

00:08:51.310 --> 00:08:53.580
cameras, that's
where you need man.

00:08:53.630 --> 00:08:56.580
You need man up there when you
need some freewheeling judgment.

00:08:56.630 --> 00:08:59.860
And the tasks in space that
should be assigned to man are

00:08:59.910 --> 00:09:03.860
the tasks that require him to
use his mind and his abilities,

00:09:03.910 --> 00:09:06.900
his unique abilities, which
certainly can't be covered by

00:09:06.950 --> 00:09:07.940
programming a computer.

00:09:07.990 --> 00:09:09.290
The

00:09:11.840 --> 00:09:15.130
reason for having a complete
package of medical experiments

00:09:15.180 --> 00:09:19.570
on Skylab, as opposed to the
case in the earlier missions

00:09:19.620 --> 00:09:22.670
where we did our medical stuff
primarily before flight and then

00:09:22.720 --> 00:09:26.010
after flight, was that we were
going to fly much longer than we

00:09:26.060 --> 00:09:27.360
ever had before.

00:09:27.760 --> 00:09:31.810
We were going to quadruple the
amount of time spent by a human

00:09:31.860 --> 00:09:35.570
being in space from
14 days to 56 days.

00:09:35.620 --> 00:09:38.290
And as it turned out, we did
better than that and went 84

00:09:38.340 --> 00:09:40.300
days on the third flight.

00:09:40.350 --> 00:09:44.540
The system we expected to change
fastest in the body was the

00:09:44.590 --> 00:09:47.680
cardiovascular system, the
heart, the blood vessels, the

00:09:47.730 --> 00:09:49.850
amount of blood in them
and the way they operated.

00:09:50.390 --> 00:09:54.960
So our number one experiment to
stress that system was called

00:09:55.010 --> 00:09:56.430
lower body negative pressure.

00:09:56.600 --> 00:09:59.380
Well, we didn't have gravity up
there so we couldn't stand up,

00:09:59.440 --> 00:10:02.150
and that lower body negative
pressure is a substitute for

00:10:02.200 --> 00:10:04.100
gravity, a way to
fake the body out.

00:10:04.450 --> 00:10:06.660
And we ran that at four day
intervals throughout the

00:10:06.710 --> 00:10:09.680
mission, and from the very first
time we ran it, it was obvious

00:10:09.730 --> 00:10:11.140
that there were indeed changes.

00:10:11.190 --> 00:10:14.870
But the encouraging thing was
that these symptoms were present

00:10:14.920 --> 00:10:18.190
the very first time we did the
test and they didn't get worse

00:10:18.240 --> 00:10:19.640
as time went on.

00:10:19.950 --> 00:10:25.180
There was another major dynamic
test or stress test that we used

00:10:25.230 --> 00:10:30.300
to determine our condition in
flight and that was the bicycle

00:10:30.350 --> 00:10:31.370
ergometer.

00:10:31.420 --> 00:10:34.570
You simply worked your heart,
your muscles and your lungs to

00:10:34.620 --> 00:10:37.890
the maximum capacity to see
whether they all played together

00:10:37.940 --> 00:10:39.850
the same way in the
absence of gravity.

00:10:39.900 --> 00:10:44.440
We found that our ability to
exercise, our heart's ability to

00:10:44.490 --> 00:10:47.920
move blood around the body, the
cells and muscles' ability to

00:10:47.970 --> 00:10:51.120
extract oxygen from it and all
the processes that go into a

00:10:51.170 --> 00:10:54.470
maximum exercise were completely
unaffected by zero gravity.

00:10:55.170 --> 00:10:58.640
It was also considered important
to check the response of the

00:10:58.690 --> 00:11:01.360
vestibular system or the little
balancing mechanism within the

00:11:01.410 --> 00:11:02.710
inner ear.

00:11:02.770 --> 00:11:04.990
And we did this by means
of a rotating chair.

00:11:05.740 --> 00:11:08.250
Now normally when you sit in
this chair and rotate on Earth

00:11:08.300 --> 00:11:11.410
and move your head, It doesn't
take very long before you

00:11:11.460 --> 00:11:13.020
experience motion sickness.

00:11:13.430 --> 00:11:15.880
However, up there we noticed
that after we were there a few

00:11:15.930 --> 00:11:18.760
days, it was possible to sit in
this chair and rotate almost all

00:11:18.810 --> 00:11:22.290
day long, with no sensation
or motion sickness at all.

00:11:25.740 --> 00:11:27.950
We took blood samples
periodically throughout the

00:11:28.000 --> 00:11:30.690
flight, mainly to make sure
that the hemoglobin level in the

00:11:30.740 --> 00:11:34.350
blood was maintained at a proper
level and that the body was

00:11:34.400 --> 00:11:38.510
responding properly to lowered
levels of hemoglobin or red

00:11:38.560 --> 00:11:40.950
blood cells us to make sure
that there was not some sort of

00:11:41.000 --> 00:11:43.650
subtle effect taking place in
zero gravity that would leave us

00:11:43.700 --> 00:11:46.660
more or less open
to infection, say.

00:11:49.270 --> 00:11:52.720
On our mission, we elected prior
to the launch to increase the

00:11:52.770 --> 00:11:56.480
exercise period from an hour to
an hour and a half, and this was

00:11:56.530 --> 00:11:59.580
done mainly on the basis of
recommendations of the previous

00:11:59.630 --> 00:12:00.930
crew.

00:12:01.340 --> 00:12:04.730
They felt there just was not
enough time for a good exercise

00:12:04.780 --> 00:12:06.800
period, that you really
needed more time.

00:12:07.040 --> 00:12:10.150
We were also bringing up the
little treadmill, which was

00:12:10.200 --> 00:12:13.250
going to require time for you,
so we just ended up going for an

00:12:13.300 --> 00:12:15.080
hour and a half
per day exercise.

00:12:15.530 --> 00:12:18.800
It appears that that decision
was a good one, because we came

00:12:18.850 --> 00:12:21.240
back, I think, in better
condition than has any other

00:12:21.290 --> 00:12:22.590
crew.

00:12:23.520 --> 00:12:26.510
The Skylab medical experiments
were perhaps one of the most

00:12:26.560 --> 00:12:29.440
important things we did during
the whole Skylab mission.

00:12:29.670 --> 00:12:32.920
We attempted to take a look at
man as he reacted under long

00:12:32.970 --> 00:12:34.270
-term weightlessness.

00:12:34.410 --> 00:12:37.980
Whether or not we can
extrapolate that 84 days out to

00:12:38.030 --> 00:12:40.160
several hundred days,
Incidentally, it takes something

00:12:40.210 --> 00:12:44.480
like 400 days for a typical Mars
mission, we don't know right

00:12:44.530 --> 00:12:48.370
now, but it does indicate that
we're on the right way to,

00:12:49.230 --> 00:12:53.980
and assuming that man can exist
for long times at zero -g.

00:13:11.510 --> 00:13:14.410
The sun turns out to be a
giant astrophysical laboratory.

00:13:14.670 --> 00:13:19.100
It is made up of plasma, that's
high temperature gases which we

00:13:19.150 --> 00:13:22.710
plan to use down here for many
schemes in energy production,

00:13:29.470 --> 00:13:32.200
and we can use the sun as a
laboratory to better understand

00:13:32.250 --> 00:13:37.060
how plasmas are affected by
electric and magnetic fields,

00:13:37.160 --> 00:13:39.360
how they're moved, what
their properties are.

00:13:40.830 --> 00:13:43.820
There were a number of really
fascinating views available from

00:13:43.870 --> 00:13:46.400
Skylab in the area of the solar
studies, some of them seen for

00:13:46.450 --> 00:13:48.330
the very first
time by human eye.

00:13:48.590 --> 00:13:51.840
In this case there was an
eruptive prominence on the disk

00:13:51.890 --> 00:13:55.500
of the sun which cast out
through the solar atmosphere

00:13:55.550 --> 00:13:59.780
called the corona to very, very
high altitudes, an expanding gas

00:13:59.830 --> 00:14:00.340
bubble.

00:14:00.390 --> 00:14:04.140
This was moving out through the
corona at a velocity of about

00:14:04.190 --> 00:14:06.170
300 miles per second.

00:14:06.370 --> 00:14:11.280
And we brought back a number of
very fascinating views of these

00:14:11.330 --> 00:14:12.630
sorts of events.

00:14:12.920 --> 00:14:15.390
Well, on our flight, we were
fortunate enough to be able to,

00:14:15.440 --> 00:14:18.290
first of all, record a solar
flare from the very beginning

00:14:18.340 --> 00:14:20.580
all the way through
the rise to the end.

00:14:20.740 --> 00:14:23.870
Based upon the previous
experience of the other crews,

00:14:23.920 --> 00:14:26.490
we were able to figure out what
happens before a flare, then

00:14:26.540 --> 00:14:29.490
beyond target and getting
data as the flare came up.

00:14:29.540 --> 00:14:33.670
We also know that when these
large flares erupt on the Sun,

00:14:33.720 --> 00:14:37.710
they throw out charged particles
which reach the Earth in some 24

00:14:37.760 --> 00:14:40.860
to 48 hours, and at least
produce aurora, which we see

00:14:40.910 --> 00:14:42.210
here on Earth.

00:14:44.720 --> 00:14:48.190
Well, the observation of comet
Kohatek offered an unprecedented

00:14:48.240 --> 00:14:49.540
opportunity.

00:14:49.680 --> 00:14:53.100
It was very bright, and most
notable was a sunward spike,

00:14:53.150 --> 00:14:55.560
which was in the opposite
direction of the tail, very

00:14:55.610 --> 00:14:57.180
sharp, and pointed
towards the Sun.

00:14:57.230 --> 00:15:01.400
And comets are important to
science because we are able to

00:15:01.450 --> 00:15:04.310
observe what we think
is primordial matter.

00:15:04.480 --> 00:15:08.230
That is, matter which has been
around since birth of the solar

00:15:08.280 --> 00:15:12.540
system, if the comet came from
the solar system, or if it came

00:15:12.590 --> 00:15:15.140
from outside the solar system,
then it's an opportunity to

00:15:15.190 --> 00:15:17.520
observe something which was not
created with our solar system

00:15:17.570 --> 00:15:20.230
but in the regular
stellar scheme of things.

00:15:21.400 --> 00:15:24.350
We also had several smaller
experiments intended to look at

00:15:24.400 --> 00:15:27.770
the ultraviolet radiation coming
from stars throughout the galaxy

00:15:27.820 --> 00:15:30.670
in which we're in, as well as
radiation coming even from other

00:15:30.720 --> 00:15:31.710
galaxies.

00:15:31.760 --> 00:15:34.750
And these experiments were
extended out the scientific

00:15:34.800 --> 00:15:38.630
airlock of the Skylab and looked
at the ultraviolet radiation

00:15:38.680 --> 00:15:41.610
coming from the sun, allowed
it to be dispersed so the image

00:15:41.660 --> 00:15:44.310
actually looks like it has a
long tail on it, and then he can

00:15:44.360 --> 00:15:47.560
see just what radiation is
coming from each of these stars.

00:15:53.590 --> 00:15:57.240
Out the three manned missions,
flight crews evaluated the day

00:15:57.290 --> 00:16:01.040
-to -day activities of living
to see what might be improved in

00:16:01.090 --> 00:16:02.810
future spacecraft design.

00:16:13.020 --> 00:16:17.010
Mobility was, I like to refer
to it as the Peter Pan mode.

00:16:17.060 --> 00:16:19.490
You could really just
push off and move around.

00:16:19.540 --> 00:16:22.210
Within the workshop, the thing
that surprised me most was how

00:16:22.260 --> 00:16:25.920
quickly we all adapted
to being fairly precise.

00:16:26.520 --> 00:16:29.250
It's very much like the
sensation you get when you're

00:16:29.300 --> 00:16:33.030
skin diving or just floating
around in the water.

00:16:33.190 --> 00:16:38.440
Well, as far as working in zero
gravity and moving yourself, it

00:16:38.490 --> 00:16:42.350
was a delight in comparison to
1G on the surface of the Earth.

00:16:42.860 --> 00:16:47.300
Once your eyes see something,
then you relate to it and you

00:16:47.350 --> 00:16:50.460
say, whatever is above my head
is up, whatever is below my feet

00:16:50.510 --> 00:16:51.180
is down.

00:16:51.230 --> 00:16:53.090
It doesn't matter whether
it's the floor or the ceiling.

00:16:54.060 --> 00:16:57.570
Every evening, after dinner and
all the work was done, we always

00:16:57.620 --> 00:17:01.250
had to take a trip or two around
the water ring lockers or just

00:17:01.300 --> 00:17:02.870
go up there and free fly.

00:17:02.920 --> 00:17:04.220
We

00:17:06.080 --> 00:17:10.630
also found that we could have
quite a bit of fun doing tricks

00:17:10.680 --> 00:17:14.870
and stunts and flips and rolls
that weren't possible here on

00:17:14.920 --> 00:17:16.220
Earth.

00:17:41.980 --> 00:17:45.930
Food aboard Skylab was a very
good treat when compared to

00:17:45.980 --> 00:17:47.350
earlier space missions.

00:17:47.400 --> 00:17:49.850
Probably the best feature was
the fact that we had the ability

00:17:49.900 --> 00:17:54.240
through a warming tray to put
all of our food out and heat it

00:17:54.290 --> 00:17:57.400
so that when you sat down to
eat, you could eat a variety of

00:17:57.450 --> 00:17:58.160
foods all at once.

00:17:58.210 --> 00:18:01.590
Probably the favorite of
everybody's up there was ice

00:18:01.640 --> 00:18:02.940
cream and strawberries.

00:18:04.400 --> 00:18:06.670
Of course, it was necessary to
clean house up there once in a

00:18:06.720 --> 00:18:07.960
while, too.

00:18:08.010 --> 00:18:11.100
All of our air was filtered
through a big screen within the

00:18:11.150 --> 00:18:12.450
orbital workshop.

00:18:12.560 --> 00:18:14.950
And as the air went through this
screen, little particles would

00:18:15.000 --> 00:18:18.450
collect on the screen of small
items that needed to be vacuumed

00:18:18.500 --> 00:18:21.820
up. And so every day or two, we
would vacuum off the screens.

00:18:22.970 --> 00:18:26.240
We found housekeeping to be not
as difficult as here on Earth.

00:18:26.290 --> 00:18:29.790
One reason was that we had a
planned place for every item.

00:18:30.750 --> 00:18:34.420
Personal hygiene wise, it was
necessary for us to perform up

00:18:34.470 --> 00:18:36.730
there about the
same way it is here.

00:18:37.360 --> 00:18:40.460
We brushed our teeth, we combed
our hair, we shaved, we washed.

00:18:40.930 --> 00:18:42.230
We did have a shower.

00:18:42.630 --> 00:18:45.960
We found, however, that we could
almost keep clean enough by

00:18:46.010 --> 00:18:49.490
using sponge baths, by washing
ourselves off with a wash rag.

00:18:49.730 --> 00:18:52.160
Although we did use the shower
on several occasions, and I

00:18:52.210 --> 00:18:55.270
think for future space stations,
the shower would be a good idea.

00:18:56.310 --> 00:19:00.180
We slept in a sleeping bag -like
device, which had straps across

00:19:00.510 --> 00:19:03.920
So once you were in the sleeping
bag and all snugged up, so to

00:19:03.970 --> 00:19:06.560
speak, well, you felt very close
to what you feel like in bed on

00:19:06.610 --> 00:19:07.910
Earth.

00:19:09.660 --> 00:19:12.090
We had on board, of course, E
-Rep, which is an abbreviation

00:19:12.140 --> 00:19:14.420
for Earth Resources
Experiments Package.

00:19:15.120 --> 00:19:18.350
The primary purpose of this
package was to evaluate the

00:19:18.400 --> 00:19:21.050
techniques and equipment to be
used in studying the Earth's

00:19:21.100 --> 00:19:22.370
surface.

00:19:22.420 --> 00:19:25.870
The Earth Resources
multispectral camera consists of

00:19:25.920 --> 00:19:28.610
a battery of six cameras all
pointed at the same place on the

00:19:28.660 --> 00:19:29.680
ground.

00:19:29.730 --> 00:19:31.920
Now, through using suitable
filters on the front of these

00:19:31.970 --> 00:19:35.250
cameras and putting different
kinds of film in there, some

00:19:35.300 --> 00:19:38.350
susceptible to visible light,
some that'll measure infrared

00:19:38.400 --> 00:19:41.240
light, some that'll measure
other frequencies of light.

00:19:41.320 --> 00:19:45.790
You're able to record what is
being radiated from the ground

00:19:45.840 --> 00:19:48.140
in these different
frequency ranges.

00:19:49.320 --> 00:19:51.570
There's been a lot of
significant data gathered in the

00:19:51.620 --> 00:19:53.860
area of determination of faults.

00:19:54.220 --> 00:19:57.110
The Southern California area, of
course, has been troubled with

00:19:57.160 --> 00:20:00.390
earthquakes for many, many
years, and we're just beginning

00:20:00.440 --> 00:20:03.520
to really understand the
fault structure in that area.

00:20:03.620 --> 00:20:06.730
We also have got some excellent
photography of the fault

00:20:06.780 --> 00:20:10.760
structure as it extends on down
into Baja California then over

00:20:10.810 --> 00:20:14.640
in New Zealand We got some of
the Alpine fault and we think

00:20:14.690 --> 00:20:18.340
that Possibly better knowledge
of the fault systems around the

00:20:18.390 --> 00:20:21.200
earth will give us a better
Understanding of earthquakes and

00:20:21.250 --> 00:20:25.330
we can learn to better predict
earthquakes you can imagine that

00:20:25.380 --> 00:20:27.590
if we had the ability to
forecast whether literally

00:20:27.640 --> 00:20:30.430
millions and millions of dollars
in crop savings alone would

00:20:30.480 --> 00:20:33.590
result and and it's just the
first step, of course, of

00:20:33.640 --> 00:20:34.940
weather control.

00:20:35.540 --> 00:20:38.550
We've been able to give them
three -dimensional looks at

00:20:38.600 --> 00:20:42.930
cloud formations in the areas of
cyclones, typhoons, hurricanes,

00:20:42.980 --> 00:20:43.900
and the like.

00:20:43.950 --> 00:20:46.460
We've given them some
photographic data that we think

00:20:46.510 --> 00:20:49.500
is going to answer a few
questions that have never been

00:20:49.550 --> 00:20:53.580
answered to date anyway on just
exactly how the Earth and its

00:20:53.630 --> 00:20:56.350
atmosphere interchanges
energy back and forth.

00:20:58.320 --> 00:21:01.130
Food supply is a very important
factor for all of us here at

00:21:01.180 --> 00:21:03.910
home, not only in this country,
but for the entire world, of

00:21:03.960 --> 00:21:04.690
course.

00:21:04.740 --> 00:21:07.870
First of all, we can survey the
amount of land that is devoted

00:21:07.920 --> 00:21:09.960
to the production
of these crops.

00:21:10.240 --> 00:21:13.030
We can even estimate what the
yield is going to be, depending

00:21:13.080 --> 00:21:15.760
upon what its temperature
and rainfall and so on is.

00:21:15.900 --> 00:21:18.820
Also, we can see where there
may be areas of infestation.

00:21:19.410 --> 00:21:21.940
These instruments have
photographed the Earth in a

00:21:21.990 --> 00:21:24.760
variety of different spectral
ranges, and clear down into the

00:21:24.810 --> 00:21:25.600
infrared.

00:21:25.650 --> 00:21:28.380
In other words, some disease,
like a corn blight, may have

00:21:28.430 --> 00:21:28.820
been started.

00:21:28.870 --> 00:21:32.060
We can see areas where the
forests have had significant

00:21:32.110 --> 00:21:33.320
infestations.

00:21:33.370 --> 00:21:36.380
So this all goes together to
help us put together a better

00:21:36.430 --> 00:21:39.840
pattern of the total world
production of food and other

00:21:39.890 --> 00:21:42.980
things of interest to people
in this country and overseas as

00:21:43.030 --> 00:21:44.330
well.

00:21:44.670 --> 00:21:47.020
We took a good look at
the Falkland Current.

00:21:47.070 --> 00:21:49.080
We could see some of the
staining, this plankton

00:21:49.130 --> 00:21:51.640
blooming, the little organisms
that grow essentially in the

00:21:51.690 --> 00:21:52.360
deep water.

00:21:52.410 --> 00:21:55.800
And then when the water wells
up from below, it brings up this

00:21:55.850 --> 00:21:58.940
green plankton, and from space
it looks like a green stain.

00:21:58.990 --> 00:22:02.890
The plankton is sort of the
bread of the sea, and it starts

00:22:02.940 --> 00:22:05.490
the life cycle in the sea, and
if you can track the plankton,

00:22:05.540 --> 00:22:07.510
you can track the smaller
fishes, which are going to track

00:22:07.560 --> 00:22:10.130
the larger fishes, the food
fishes, which are good for

00:22:10.180 --> 00:22:11.480
commercial people.

00:22:14.760 --> 00:22:18.350
We took some excellent pictures
of the drought area of Central

00:22:18.400 --> 00:22:22.150
Africa, trying to discover the
total pattern of the drought, to

00:22:22.200 --> 00:22:26.320
see if it could be stopped in
some way by suitable planting,

00:22:26.720 --> 00:22:29.550
or suitable cultivation, or
preventing cultivation of

00:22:29.600 --> 00:22:30.110
certain areas.

00:22:30.160 --> 00:22:33.010
So although we don't know the
full techniques that would be

00:22:33.060 --> 00:22:36.430
used in this case, at least we
can get a sort of a handle on

00:22:36.480 --> 00:22:39.600
food production and
enhance it in some way.

00:22:42.360 --> 00:22:45.270
As far as man -made pollution,
I think that probably the most

00:22:45.320 --> 00:22:48.480
outstanding visual evidence
of this is in the Great Lakes.

00:22:48.780 --> 00:22:51.670
And it starts at the source of
the Great Lakes, the western end

00:22:51.720 --> 00:22:54.050
of Lake Superior, where you
can see pollutants along the

00:22:54.100 --> 00:22:55.230
southern shore of Lake Erie.

00:22:55.280 --> 00:22:58.230
You can see where every large
creek or small river empties

00:22:58.280 --> 00:22:59.580
into the lake.

00:23:00.160 --> 00:23:04.210
We noticed in areas like
Mobile Bay and the mouth of the

00:23:04.260 --> 00:23:08.170
Mississippi River and several
other river areas around it You

00:23:08.220 --> 00:23:11.830
could see the silt moving out
into the the various harbors and

00:23:11.880 --> 00:23:15.110
bays around the world Part of
this I think we can probably

00:23:15.160 --> 00:23:18.630
blame on man because of poor
soil conservation practices

00:23:18.680 --> 00:23:23.080
upstream Much easier to take
a picture or to look down from

00:23:23.130 --> 00:23:25.960
orbit and try to survey the
situation and find the areas

00:23:26.010 --> 00:23:29.630
most likely to be sites for
future oil fields for example.

00:23:29.960 --> 00:23:33.330
We think that this technique
is going to make quite a lot of

00:23:33.380 --> 00:23:36.710
energy sources available that
just haven't been discovered yet

00:23:36.760 --> 00:23:39.120
because of the vast
surface of the earth.

00:23:55.340 --> 00:24:00.100
The earth, the sun, the stars,
prime targets for Skylab

00:24:00.150 --> 00:24:01.450
investigations.

00:24:02.750 --> 00:24:05.940
But within the workshop too,
experiments and demonstrations

00:24:05.990 --> 00:24:08.800
covered a broad range of
science and technology.

00:24:08.850 --> 00:24:10.150
In

00:24:37.970 --> 00:24:42.460
early February 1974, the third
crew retrieved, for the last

00:24:42.510 --> 00:24:47.020
time, the film, magnetic tape,
the samples and specimens for

00:24:47.070 --> 00:24:48.370
return to Earth.

00:24:48.940 --> 00:24:52.860
The operational phase of Skylab
was nearing a successful end.

00:24:53.870 --> 00:24:57.600
The ongoing science phase,
fueled by the aggregate of data

00:24:57.650 --> 00:25:01.430
from all three missions, would
last for the next several years.

00:25:16.480 --> 00:25:17.780
I

00:26:04.010 --> 00:26:07.200
don't think there's any real
limit to how long man can stay

00:26:07.250 --> 00:26:10.660
in space as long as he never
loses sight of the fact that

00:26:10.710 --> 00:26:13.190
sometimes he's going to
have to go back to 1G.

00:26:16.470 --> 00:26:19.800
And if he keeps that in mind and
keeps his cardiovascular system

00:26:19.850 --> 00:26:23.730
and his muscles and his bones
toned for that eventuality, then

00:26:23.780 --> 00:26:26.390
there's no reason to believe
that man need worry about how

00:26:26.440 --> 00:26:27.570
long he spends in space.

00:26:27.620 --> 00:26:28.750
I don't think there's
a limit to that.

00:26:28.800 --> 00:26:33.590
We all know that the Earth's
resources are limited, and in

00:26:33.640 --> 00:26:37.710
order to enjoy the benefits of
the resources of the Earth that

00:26:37.760 --> 00:26:40.270
we have enjoyed in the past,
we're going to have to learn how

00:26:40.320 --> 00:26:42.300
to manage what we
have more efficiently.

00:26:42.990 --> 00:26:46.540
We're going to have to learn how
to find new resources to improve

00:26:46.590 --> 00:26:48.330
our quality of
life here on Earth.

00:26:50.000 --> 00:26:53.090
The solar physics community
has about five years of very

00:26:53.140 --> 00:26:54.570
intensive work ahead of them.

00:26:54.620 --> 00:26:57.170
We have brought back so many
thousands of pictures, and this

00:26:57.220 --> 00:27:00.070
is actually upon the order of
100,000 photographs now of the

00:27:00.120 --> 00:27:03.310
sun, which they have to look
through and analyze in order to

00:27:03.360 --> 00:27:06.510
better understand all of this
activity that's going on in the

00:27:06.560 --> 00:27:07.860
sun.

00:27:10.100 --> 00:27:13.610
Also, we have operated very
extensive and complex equipment

00:27:13.660 --> 00:27:17.330
in Skylab and established that
this is a viable operation and

00:27:17.380 --> 00:27:18.780
that you can do it.

00:27:18.940 --> 00:27:23.010
We have learned from Skylab
exactly what features are best,

00:27:23.060 --> 00:27:25.910
what features to avoid in
the way of designing, say, a

00:27:25.960 --> 00:27:28.890
spacecraft or space station
itself or even certain pieces of

00:27:28.940 --> 00:27:30.540
scientific equipment. I

00:27:38.800 --> 00:27:41.570
don't think anybody really knows
what man's future is at the

00:27:41.620 --> 00:27:42.330
moment.

00:27:42.380 --> 00:27:46.220
However, we do know that what
man's been capable of doing, and

00:27:46.270 --> 00:27:48.720
not only has he always done,
he's always done it to his

00:27:48.770 --> 00:27:49.830
advantage.

00:27:49.880 --> 00:27:52.090
So I think maybe space
exploration is just the

00:27:52.140 --> 00:27:56.650
beginning of man being able to
go out to other areas of the

00:27:56.700 --> 00:27:57.650
universe.

00:27:57.700 --> 00:27:59.730
And once he's able to go out
there, he's going to find uses

00:27:59.780 --> 00:28:01.730
for it, and he's going to go out
there and live, and he's going

00:28:01.780 --> 00:28:04.350
to populate it, and he's going
to make a better life for

00:28:04.400 --> 00:28:08.140
himself and for the rest of the
people of the Earth in doing so.
